# Never commit build output or credentials.
config.json
server-secret.txt
*.7z
*.zip
*.exe
_dist/
published.json

# Copies of the server's own logs, pulled down by tools\Get-Logs.ps1 to be
# read. They belong to whichever run made them, not to the repo.
logs/
*.log

# Scratch that has been committed by accident before.
tools/thornswood-probe.js

# The Discord webhook is a credential: anyone holding the URL can post as the
# bot. Publish-Discord reads it from here on the machine that ships.
tools/discord/news-webhook.txt

# Scratch beside the repo, and the local patch runs that made 1.1.35.
_to_delete/

# Python's bytecode cache. Running the bot once leaves tools\discord\__pycache__
# behind, and it then shows up as untracked in every git status after, which is
# how it nearly went into an unrelated pull request on 2026-09-17.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# What Publish-Discord.ps1 has already announced, one line per channel. Not a
# credential, and not shared either: the two builds announce different
# versions and each shipping machine has its own history, so a tracked copy
# would be a conflict on every release. Ship commits nothing now, but it used
# to commit the whole working tree, which is the other reason this stays out.
tools/discord/announced.json

# Which ticket each person has open, written by the bot beside itself on the
# VPS. It is a list of the Discord ids of everybody waiting on staff, so it
# stays out of the repo if the bot is ever run from a checkout.
tools/discord/open-tickets.json

# The dev build's own news webhook, for the dev updates channel. Same rule as
# news-webhook.txt above: anyone holding the URL can post as the bot.
tools/discord/dev-webhook.txt

# Where a certificate lives and how to drive it. Never a password or a key,
# but it names a machine's own signing setup and only that machine needs it.
# tools\Sign-Artifact.ps1 does nothing when this is absent, which is how a
# machine without a certificate still builds.
tools/signing.json
# and any copy of it kept beside it while the setup is being changed.
tools/signing.json.*

# The passphrase the nightly backups are encrypted with. Restore-Backup.ps1
# looks for it beside itself, so the name has to be unable to land in here.
# Where it really lives is Documents on Patrick's machine and
# C:\Thornswood-Backup on the VPS, and it must be in both.
tools/backup-key.txt
thornswood-backup-key.txt

# What the voice service makes beside itself the first time it runs. On the VPS
# it lives in its own folder, so these only appear here when somebody runs it
# out of a checkout to try something, which is exactly when nobody is watching
# git status: token-key.txt signs the join tokens, secret.txt is the password
# the account routes check, and owners.json is the register it keeps. Found on
# 23 September by running the service locally to prove a header, which left a
# signing key sitting untracked in tools\voice.
tools/voice/token-key.txt
tools/voice/secret.txt
tools/voice/owners.json
tools/voice/owners.json.tmp
tools/voice/chat/
